175
00:22:12,960 --> 00:22:28,400
So let us say we want to vary the regulation parameter between 0 and let us say 5 in 50 steps.

176
00:22:37,120 --> 00:22:42,800
We stick with the same cross-validation evaluation and execute.

177
00:22:49,440 --> 00:22:54,720
The result of the execution of the meta learning algorithm shows the optimal

178
00:22:54,720 --> 00:23:02,080
value of the regularization parameter as follows subject to the range constraints provided by us.

179
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:07,840
The β parameters and the corresponding error measures are shown here.

180
00:23:09,200 --> 00:23:13,520
Comparing this result with the results of the previous two executions,

181
00:23:15,920 --> 00:23:21,840
where the regularization parameter was set to 0 and 0.5,

182
00:23:24,400 --> 00:23:27,280
we see that there are small changes but not nothing drastic.

183
00:23:29,360 --> 00:23:33,920
This seems to suggest that regulation does not seem to have much effect

184
00:23:33,920 --> 00:23:38,320
on this model or perhaps that we have not found the right range of parameters.

185
00:23:40,640 --> 00:23:50,240
In case of the latter we can run the meta learning algorithm again and specify a larger range.

186
00:23:50,240 --> 00:23:54,560
One very useful technique when searching for the optimal parameters

187
00:23:54,560 --> 00:24:01,280
for any learning algorithm is to start with a large range and the large step size. This

188
00:24:01,280 --> 00:24:08,080
initial step performs a coarse-grained search over the range of parameter values.

189
00:24:09,120 --> 00:24:14,000
Next we perform a fine-grained search in the vicinity of the value which gave the

190
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:20,480
best results in the previous step. That is we restrict the range but decrease the step size.

191
00:24:22,160 --> 00:24:26,880
We can leave it to you to apply these two-stage parameters search on this data set

192
